\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The half-turn commutant permits mixing within a sector}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{For the real [half-turn](fdip-000L), define its commutant by
#{\operatorname{Comm}(P)=\{R\in\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(V):RP=PR\}}.
Restriction gives an algebra isomorphism
##{\operatorname{Comm}(P)\longrightarrow
\operatorname{End}(V_+)\times\operatorname{End}(V_-),\qquad
R\longmapsto(R|_{V_+},R|_{V_-}).}
The group-algebra image consists exactly of pairs
#{(xI_{V_+},yI_{V_-})} and is strictly smaller than this commutant.}

\proof{
\p{If #{RP=PR} and #{Pv=\varepsilon v}, then
#{PRv=RPv=\varepsilon Rv}, so #{R} preserves each sector.
Conversely, a map preserving both sectors satisfies
#{PR(v_++v_-)=Rv_+-Rv_-=RP(v_++v_-)}.
The inverse to restriction acts separately on the unique sector
components. Composition and the unit are componentwise, proving
the algebra isomorphism.}

\p{The map #{aI+bP} restricts to scalars #{x=a+b} and #{y=a-b}.
Every pair occurs using #{a=(x+y)/2} and #{b=(x-y)/2}.
Choose the explicit sector bases
#{(b_{+,0},b_{+,1})} and #{(b_{-,0},b_{-,1})} from the
[half-turn sector construction](fdip-000L). The map swapping
#{b_{+,0},b_{+,1}} and vanishing on #{V_-} preserves both sectors,
so it belongs to the commutant. It is not scalar on #{V_+}, proving
strict containment.}
}

\p{Each sign occurs twice, so an operator commuting with #{P} may
mix the two vectors in either sector. Symmetry therefore does not
force #{Q_\pm} to be scalar; the
[cycle precision blocks](fdip-003S) exhibit the freedom explicitly.
The [basis realization](fdip-00KL) retains the complete array
calculation. The group-algebra/module convention comes from
\citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations};
the commutant calculation is direct. The
[interpretation boundary](fgap-0013) remains: this finite algebra
calculation selects no continuous gauge group, physical mass spectrum
or interaction.}
